   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network, where they are received by your router.\n\nThe router acts as a central hub, directing traffic to the appropriate devices. It connects to your internet service provider (ISP) and manages the flow of data to and from your devices. Once the video packets reach your router, the router forwards them to the access point, which is often the same device as the router itself or a separate Wi-Fi access point. The access point then broadcasts the signal over one of the available radio channels, typically using either 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz frequencies.\n\nThe laptop receives the video packets through its Wi-Fi adapter, which decodes the signal and reassembles the packets into the original video stream. This process is managed by the Wi-Fi protocol, which ensures that the packets are delivered in the correct order and without errors.\n\nWhen two devices transmit at once, a phenomenon known as a collision can occur. In a Wi-Fi"
